#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135045 :tutorial/content "A set of vectors ${U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is an **orthogonal set** if each pair of distinct vectors from the set is orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following set:\n\n![](images/9b/9b5afbbe850cb936b16b177e0a1b42bdd94df9bdc5dd3830aadc0de328a2b0d8.svg)\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${W}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{w}_1, \\mathbf{w}_2, \\mathbf{w}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${W}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${W}$ is an orthogonal set.\n\nIf an orthogonal set is also a basis, then we call it an **orthogonal basis**. Here, $W$ is an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ because it contains three linearly independent (you can check this) orthogonal vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "dd93c4b2-6ec7-5a9c-a669-7979210393dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3618 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135090 :tutorial/content "A set of vectors ![](images/96/969c7c332d8285fa0419e716cad301755b3ff1f9f074ab55798ef7c6ea09b8a1.svg) is an **orthonormal set** if it is an orthogonal set of *unit* vectors. Remember that ![](images/25/257a1924bd39970f89e8a9becbac5a5f260572a7e181dedc91ebcc4bb04b0362.svg) is a unit vector if ![](images/e5/e5ae26b69002c7beb7ee09911c2f5da8f62cd1c4ab687657a7e995d89c3f319a.svg) or equivalently, ![](images/d8/d8b5f1f6c4f3c8955cffeac4e7a4407b0d7a5e26afbf95d87e6dd71cc83c0c47.svg)\n\nAn orthonormal set that is also a basis, like the standard basis, is called an **orthonormal basis**. For instance, the standard basis ![](images/f8/f80114a44b31082d68abe17a7d45f60a6455a147a82001ef548eaed69c0c609d.svg) of ![](images/46/4607eb52aa0195587414ef27616970a6b47226a3bb476e944ac079422dc1e1b0.svg) is an orthonormal basis because the vectors are orthogonal to each other and each vector in the set is a unit vector.\n\nThere are two more things to note about orthogonal sets:\n\n- Any orthogonal set of non-zero vectors is linearly independent.\n\n- An orthogonal set in ![](images/5b/5b243567f6e2c78e286689189eb1336948b7e67d92e7c15472ca77e9fcff3bb0.svg) cannot contain more than ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) vectors. More precisely, if ![](images/59/5927da2243d5e3815e5201f00b72ebe3eae6119ba048eaf7a0505c5e1fddffbc.svg) is an orthogonal set of non-zero vectors and ![](images/6b/6b4f5fffc4d51993128d1ad6ebdbf0c543dad75a0295656991f927ed23ae3714.svg) then we must have that ![](images/15/15cac3b851d0f5feb26cd7d31c10fc21eac370c2addcdbd8634030f85bc3323b.svg)\n\n**Note:** To remember that an orthogonal set of non-zero vectors in ![](images/5b/5b243567f6e2c78e286689189eb1336948b7e67d92e7c15472ca77e9fcff3bb0.svg) cannot contain more than ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) vectors, you can use the following intuitive mnemonic. There are only ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) dimensions in ![](images/55/552bf86a483b6d2163ddc2f4ed2748c55cb24e34b4687567631f9a92db8bc158.svg) and each non-zero orthogonal vector has a ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg)-dimensional span, meaning that it \"takes up\" ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) dimension of ![](images/9c/9cc82a4b9e5c3344d1b66c539046dd27eeb1a53aadd2beba64562c3b4053e84e.svg) So, you can't \"fit\" more than ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) orthogonal vectors in ![](images/9c/9cc82a4b9e5c3344d1b66c539046dd27eeb1a53aadd2beba64562c3b4053e84e.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "85873149-e58a-571e-8f5f-08cb9460ee47" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4353 :tutorial/title "Orthonormal Sets and Orthonormal Bases"}]}
